# The ledger of `poll`: what a program can do with readiness, timers and a
# wait over many of them, one block per capability, and what Rebel answers.
# The lists are this machine's, 2026-11-16: Python 3.15's `selectors` and
# `select` (`dir()` of each module, of `DefaultSelector`, of a `select.poll()`
# object and of `select.kqueue`), Go 1.27's `go doc syscall` (darwin) and
# `go doc time`; Go's own network poller is internal to its runtime, and a Go
# program reaches it through `net.Conn`'s blocking calls and deadlines, which
# the `go` lines name. Rust's standard library has no readiness and no timer
# wheel; the author's request named `tokio`, so the `rust` lines are `tokio`'s
# and `mio`'s public items, read from their documentation as capabilities (no
# crate source is on this machine, and none was opened).

capability poll/set  a set of things waited on together, made and closed
  heroes  poll.make, poll.close
  go      syscall.Kqueue, syscall.Close
  rust    mio::Poll::new, mio::Poll::registry, tokio::runtime::Runtime (the reactor inside it)
  python  selectors.DefaultSelector, selectors.KqueueSelector, selectors.EpollSelector, selectors.DevpollSelector, selectors.PollSelector, selectors.SelectSelector, selectors.BaseSelector, BaseSelector.close, select.kqueue, select.epoll, select.poll, select.devpoll, kqueue.close, kqueue.closed
  note    a set is made with the most entries it holds, 1 to 65,535, where Python and mio grow theirs: the platform layer allocates nothing outside the runtime's allocator, so a set reserves its room at making; `limit` past it

capability poll/connection  a connection waited on for bytes to read, room to write, or both
  heroes  poll.add_conn, poll.Interest
  go      net.Conn.Read, net.Conn.Write (each waits in the runtime's poller)
  rust    mio::Registry::register, mio::Interest::READABLE, mio::Interest::WRITABLE, tokio::net::TcpStream::ready, tokio::net::TcpStream::readable, tokio::net::TcpStream::writable, tokio::io::Interest
  python  BaseSelector.register, selectors.EVENT_READ, selectors.EVENT_WRITE, poll.register, select.POLLIN, select.POLLOUT, select.POLLRDNORM, select.POLLWRNORM, select.KQ_FILTER_READ, select.KQ_FILTER_WRITE, select.KQ_EV_ADD, select.kevent
  note    an interest is a variant of three cases, `read`, `write`, `read_write`, where Python and mio take a mask: a mask of none is a registration that waits for nothing, which Python refuses at run time and this refuses by its type

capability poll/listener  a listener waited on for a connection to accept
  heroes  poll.add_listener
  go      net.Listener.Accept (waits in the runtime's poller)
  rust    mio::net::TcpListener, tokio::net::TcpListener::accept
  python  BaseSelector.register (a listening socket, EVENT_READ)
  note    a listener is told `readable`, as all three tell it; the function takes no interest, a listener having nothing to write

capability poll/datagram  a datagram socket waited on for a datagram to receive or room to send
  heroes  poll.add_udp
  go      net.UDPConn.ReadFrom (waits in the runtime's poller)
  rust    mio::net::UdpSocket, tokio::net::UdpSocket::ready, tokio::net::UdpSocket::readable
  python  BaseSelector.register (a datagram socket)

capability poll/stream  a pipe, a terminal, a serial port, a local channel or a file waited on beside the sockets
  heroes  poll.add_stream
  go      os.File.Read on a pipe (waits in the runtime's poller where the system allows it)
  rust    tokio::io::unix::AsyncFd, mio::unix::SourceFd, mio::unix::pipe
  python  BaseSelector.register (any object with `fileno`), select.select, select.POLLPRI, select.POLLRDBAND, select.POLLWRBAND
  note    a regular file is ready at every wait, on every system, as POSIX's poll defines it; Python's EpollSelector refuses one (`EPERM`, epoll's own), and this answers the same on the three systems. Windows waits on sockets alone, so a pipe or a console there is `unsupported`, said by the function and by its example; `/dev/null` is refused by Darwin's kqueue (`EINVAL`, measured 2026-11-16) and by epoll, `unsupported`. A serial port, a pseudo-terminal's controlling side and a channel of `ipc` are waited on as the descriptor they read (defect 2667); a stream of `tls` is `unsupported`, its engine holding records it opened that the socket under them no longer shows, so a wait on the socket could sleep while the stream holds bytes

capability poll/change  what an entry waits for, changed in place
  heroes  poll.change
  go      -
  rust    mio::Registry::reregister
  python  BaseSelector.modify, poll.modify, epoll.modify

capability poll/remove  an entry taken out of the set
  heroes  poll.remove
  go      -
  rust    mio::Registry::deregister
  python  BaseSelector.unregister, poll.unregister, epoll.unregister, select.KQ_EV_DELETE
  note    a key removed names nothing after, and a later entry never takes it: Python keys an entry by its descriptor, which the system may give to the next file opened

capability poll/key  each entry known by a value the program keeps, carried by every event of it
  heroes  poll.add_conn, poll.Event
  go      -
  rust    mio::Token, mio::event::Event::token
  python  selectors.SelectorKey, BaseSelector.get_key, BaseSelector.get_map
  note    the set answers the key, a positive `i64`, where mio's program picks its Token and Python's keeps any `data`: a key the set issued is checked as a handle is, so a stale key is `not_found` and never another entry; a program maps keys to its connections with a `{i64: ...}`

capability poll/count  how many entries a set holds
  heroes  poll.count
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  len(BaseSelector.get_map())

capability poll/wait  wait until something is ready, at most a given time, and be told what and for what
  heroes  poll.wait, poll.Event
  go      net.Conn.SetDeadline, syscall.Kevent, syscall.Select
  rust    mio::Poll::poll, mio::Events, mio::event::Event::is_readable, mio::event::Event::is_writable, mio::event::Event::is_read_closed, mio::event::Event::is_write_closed, mio::event::Event::is_error, tokio::time::timeout, tokio::select!, tokio::io::Ready
  python  BaseSelector.select, select.select, poll.poll, epoll.poll, kqueue.control, select.POLLHUP, select.POLLERR, select.POLLNVAL, select.KQ_EV_EOF, select.KQ_EV_ERROR
  note    a limit of zero asks without waiting, as Python's select(0); a limit that passes with nothing ready answers an empty list, as all three do, and is no failure. At most 1,024 events at once, the rest told by the next wait. The hangup is told beside `readable` where the entry reads, so a loop that reads on `readable` meets the end of stream on every system (kqueue's `EV_EOF`, epoll's `EPOLLRDHUP` and `EPOLLHUP`, Windows' `POLLHUP`)

capability poll/wait-forever  wait with no limit
  heroes  poll.wait_forever
  go      net.Conn.Read with no deadline
  rust    mio::Poll::poll (None)
  python  BaseSelector.select (None), poll.poll (None)
  note    a function of its own rather than a sentinel limit, as README § A4 asks of an answer and this asks of an argument; it is `wait` with the largest limit

capability poll/level-triggered  what stays ready told at every wait
  heroes  poll.wait
  go      -
  rust    mio's sources are edge-triggered (a program reads until `WouldBlock`)
  python  selectors (level-triggered), select.KQ_EV_CLEAR (edge, opt-in), select.EPOLLET (edge, opt-in)
  note    Python's choice: `io`'s read waits for a byte, so a program cannot read "until it would block", which edge-triggering needs to lose nothing

capability poll/edge-triggered  told once per change of readiness
  heroes  refused: a program here cannot read until a read would block, `io`'s read waiting for a byte, so an edge-triggered set would lose what stays unread
  go      -
  rust    mio::Poll (edge-triggered by its design)
  python  select.EPOLLET, select.KQ_EV_CLEAR

capability poll/one-shot  an entry disarmed after it is told once
  heroes  refused: it exists for several threads that share one set, which a set does not allow (one call at a time, and no number names a set); `change` and `remove` do it on the one thread
  go      -
  rust    tokio's internal registration
  python  select.EPOLLONESHOT, select.KQ_EV_ONESHOT, select.KQ_EV_DISABLE, select.KQ_EV_ENABLE

capability poll/timer  a timer that falls once, at a time from now
  heroes  poll.add_timer
  go      time.NewTimer, time.After, time.Timer.Stop
  rust    tokio::time::sleep, tokio::time::sleep_until, tokio::time::Sleep
  python  select.KQ_FILTER_TIMER
  note    a timer is an entry of the set, told by `wait` with `due`, so a loop waits on its connections and its timers in one call, as `tokio::select!` over a socket and a `sleep` does; stopping one is `remove`

capability poll/interval  a timer that falls again and again at a period
  heroes  poll.add_timer
  go      time.NewTicker, time.Tick, time.Ticker.Stop
  rust    tokio::time::interval, tokio::time::interval_at, tokio::time::Interval::tick
  python  -
  note    `every` above zero; zero is a timer that falls once, so one function is both

capability poll/missed-ticks  what an interval does when the program waited past several of its times
  heroes  poll.Event
  go      time.Ticker (drops the ticks a slow receiver missed)
  rust    tokio::time::MissedTickBehavior::Burst, tokio::time::MissedTickBehavior::Delay, tokio::time::MissedTickBehavior::Skip
  python  -
  note    told once, `due` counting every time it fell, then due at the first of its times still to come: Go's and tokio's `Skip`, with the count tokio and Go do not give. Tokio's default `Burst` is not offered: a burst of events for one timer says nothing the count does not; the floor's test of it reads the layer's own clock either side of each wait since 2026-11-19 (repairs 6), an interval of 10 ms asserted told once with every time the clock allows, and told again only where the clock reached its next time: with 1 ms and no clock it failed once on a loaded Mac, 1 run in 200 under 24 busy processes and 20 in 21 with a pause of 1 to 20 ms put between two waits

capability poll/reset-timer  a timer set again from now
  heroes  poll.reset_timer
  go      time.Timer.Reset, time.Ticker.Reset
  rust    tokio::time::Sleep::reset, tokio::time::Interval::reset
  python  -

capability poll/stop  a wait ended when the program is asked to stop
  heroes  poll.wait
  go      os/signal.NotifyContext (with a select)
  rust    tokio::signal::ctrl_c (with tokio::select!)
  python  -
  note    every wait fails `stopped` once the program's stop is asked, a signal caught by `os.catch_stop` or `os.ask_stop` from any thread, even with entries ready, so a loop ends under load as `net.accept` does

capability poll/wake  a wait woken by another thread of the program, for a reason of its own
  heroes  pending
  go      a channel send seen by a select
  rust    mio::Waker, tokio::sync::Notify
  python  - (a pipe written by the other thread, registered in the selector)
  note    a set crosses to no thread, a thread being handed one `i64` (spec § 13): a waker needs a number another thread rings, as `net`'s socket numbers are, and a channel of its own per set (`EVFILT_USER`, an `eventfd`, a loopback socket on Windows); the stop, which wakes every wait, is what crosses today

capability poll/process-and-signal-filters  waiting on a process's end, a signal or a file's change in the same call
  heroes  refused: each is another package's (`process` waits for a child, `os`'s stop is the one signal a wait hears, a file's change has no package yet), and kqueue has them where epoll and Windows have none to match
  go      -
  rust    tokio::process::Child::wait, tokio::signal::unix::signal
  python  select.KQ_FILTER_PROC, select.KQ_FILTER_SIGNAL, select.KQ_FILTER_VNODE, select.KQ_FILTER_AIO, select.KQ_NOTE_ATTRIB, select.KQ_NOTE_CHILD, select.KQ_NOTE_DELETE, select.KQ_NOTE_EXEC, select.KQ_NOTE_EXIT, select.KQ_NOTE_EXTEND, select.KQ_NOTE_FORK, select.KQ_NOTE_LINK, select.KQ_NOTE_LOWAT, select.KQ_NOTE_PCTRLMASK, select.KQ_NOTE_PDATAMASK, select.KQ_NOTE_RENAME, select.KQ_NOTE_REVOKE, select.KQ_NOTE_TRACK, select.KQ_NOTE_TRACKERR, select.KQ_NOTE_WRITE

capability poll/coroutines  a function suspended while it waits and resumed when its socket is ready
  heroes  refused: coroutines and async functions are refused by the language (design.md Part 6); the program's own loop over `wait` is the form
  go      goroutines over the runtime's poller
  rust    async fn, .await, tokio::spawn, tokio::task, std::future::Future
  python  - (asyncio is out of this area)

capability poll/callback-timer  a function run when a timer falls
  heroes  refused: no closure, and no function of the program runs on a thread the library makes; a timer's fall is an event the loop acts on
  go      time.AfterFunc
  rust    -
  python  -

dropped
  python  selectors.ABCMeta, selectors.Mapping, selectors.abstractmethod, selectors.math, selectors.namedtuple, selectors.select, selectors.sys (the module's own imports); BaseSelector.fileno, kqueue.fileno, kqueue.fromfd (raw descriptors); select.PIPE_BUF (a pipe's atomic write size, `io`'s area); select.KQ_EV_FLAG1, select.KQ_EV_SYSFLAGS (kqueue's own flags); select.error (Python's alias of OSError)
  go      syscall.SetNonblock (the layer's sockets are non-blocking already), syscall.FdSet, syscall.Kevent_t, syscall.Timeval, syscall.Timeval32 (the system's structures, which no platform type crossing the layer allows)
  rust    mio::Events::with_capacity (an answer's room, which `wait` sizes itself), mio::event::Source (an interface: the language has none)
